# No polling — event-driven server communication

**Maintainer policy:** We do **not** use auto-polling (no `refetchInterval`, no `setInterval` refetch loops, no “every N seconds while panel open”).

Instead, **think through the conditions** and hit relays **only when something actually warrants a read or write**.

## Use these triggers (pick what fits the feature)

| Trigger | Example |
|---------|---------|
| **User action** | “Update fights”, “Update arena”, login, submit |
| **After our publish** | One settle delay → **one** refetch to confirm indexing (not a retry loop) |
| **Panel / tab opens** | Single `enabled: isOpen` fetch (no interval) |
| **Mutation lifecycle** | `onSuccess` / `onSettled` → invalidate or refetch once |
| **State transition** | Queue → visible opponent → publish match (joiner only) |
| **Explicit subscribe** | Nostr `req` subscription with cleanup when panel closes — only if the feature needs live updates and the user asked for it |

## Never add

- `refetchInterval` on TanStack Query for game/relay data
- Background timers that refetch “to keep the board fresh”
- Multi-attempt fetch loops inside mutations (e.g. 6× sleep-and-query) unless the user explicitly designed that retry policy

## If you think polling is needed

**Stop.** Propose **which condition** should trigger the fetch and **what the user sees** between triggers. Do not implement interval polling as a default or fallback.

## Allowed (not polling)

- **UI-only timers** (clocks, combat round pacing, animations) — no network
- **One delayed refetch** after publish (e.g. 8s timer **once** to replace optimistic rows) — not recurring
- **Bounded work inside one user gesture** that completes before the UI unlocks (single fetch after publish, not a loop)

## Legacy code

Chat and other areas may still have old `refetchInterval`. Do **not** extend that pattern. When touching those files, prefer event-driven sync unless the user specifies otherwise.

When in doubt: **manual refresh + clear status copy**, or **one fetch on the condition that changed**.
